  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Quarry and mine inspection covers three common duties: plant checks, statutory tip and tailings inspections, and site health and safety audits</li><li>Quarries are regulated under the Quarries Regulations 1999 and mines under the Mines Regulations 2014, both under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 and MHSWR</li><li>Plant such as crushers, conveyors and excavators falls under LOLER 1998 and PUWER 1998; the HSE is the regulator</li><li>Most operators never fill in a dedicated app on site; they complete it later from memory, and the record suffers</li><li>The software captures evidence and generates the report; it does not replace the competent person or the appointed geotechnical specialist</li></ul></div><h2>What quarry inspection software is actually for</h2><p>Quarries and mines generate a heavy stream of records: pre-use and periodic plant checks, statutory tip and tailings lagoon inspections, and health and safety audits of the working site. Each has its own duty holder and its own audience, from the HSE to insurers to the operator's own safety manager. What they share is that the record has to be accurate, timestamped and complete, and it has to survive scrutiny years later.</p><p>Software does not make any of these inspections valid. The competent person does. What software does is make the record easier to complete correctly on site, harder to forget an item, and faster to deliver. A good tool means the operator does not reconstruct a tip inspection from memory in the wheel loader at the end of the shift.</p><h2>The regulatory regime</h2><p>Quarries in Great Britain are regulated under the Quarries Regulations 1999, which set duties on the operator for safe design and operation, including excavation and tip stability, traffic management, dust control and health surveillance. Mines fall under the Mines Regulations 2014. Both sit under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 and the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999. The Health and Safety Executive is the regulator and enforces on site. This guide is general information, not legal advice; check the current regulations and your own site rules.</p><h2>The three common quarry and mine duties</h2><h3>1. Mobile and fixed plant inspection</h3><p>Mobile plant such as excavators, dump trucks and wheel loaders, and fixed plant such as crushers, screens and conveyors, fall under PUWER 1998, with lifting equipment and accessories under LOLER 1998. The record captures the pre-use and periodic check against each item, with photos. See the quarry plant inspection checklist for the full item list.</p><h3>2. Tip and tailings lagoon inspection</h3><p>Tips and tailings lagoons carry statutory inspection duties under the quarries regime, along with geotechnical assessment by an appointed specialist. The operator's routine inspection records the visible condition; it does not replace the specialist's geotechnical assessment. The record has to tie each observation to a date, a location on the tip and a photograph.</p><h3>3. Site health and safety audit</h3><p>A health and safety audit against the Quarries Regulations 1999 covers vehicle and pedestrian segregation, edge and edge-of-excavation protection, dust including respirable crystalline silica, signage, welfare and the safe operation of plant. The record shows what was audited, what was found and what action was raised.</p><h2>A note on dust and silica</h2><p>Quarrying generates respirable crystalline silica (RCS), a serious health hazard controlled under COSHH. Dust suppression, monitoring and health surveillance are duties on the operator. Quickler can record that controls were in place and photograph suppression and signage as evidence. It does not measure exposure or replace an occupational hygiene assessment. Check the current HSE guidance on RCS.</p><h2>What to look for in quarry inspection software</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to complete the record during the inspection, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of observation:</strong> each finding tied to a timestamped image, not a folder sorted out that evening</li><li><strong>Clean report output:</strong> a record the HSE, insurer and your own safety manager accept without explanation</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes firms for their office staff and subcontractors. Quickler charges per report, unlimited users on every bundle</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, operator, location, every observation, for the insurer or dispute that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the evidence and generates the report. It does not make an inspection valid, and it does not replace a competent person. A statutory geotechnical tip assessment still needs the appointed specialist. The competent person carries the validity. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is quarry inspection software?</h3><p>It is any tool that helps a quarry or mine operator record an inspection and produce a report: plant checks, statutory tip and tailings inspections, and health and safety audits. The options range from paper, to generic audit apps like iAuditor, to conversation-based tools like Quickler that run the workflow over WhatsApp so there is no app to install.</p><h3>What regulations govern quarry and mine inspections in the UK?</h3><p>Quarries in Great Britain are regulated under the Quarries Regulations 1999 and mines under the Mines Regulations 2014, both under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 and MHSWR. Plant is covered by PUWER 1998 and LOLER 1998. The HSE is the regulator. This is not legal advice; check the current regulations for your site.</p><h3>Can I run quarry inspections over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflows run over the WhatsApp Business API. The operator receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the completed report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Does the software replace the competent person or geotechnical specialist?</h3><p>No. Quickler captures the evidence and generates the report. It does not make an inspection valid, and a statutory geotechnical tip assessment still needs the appointed specialist. The competent person carries the judgement; the software carries the record.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/quarry-plant-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Quarry plant inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/tip-tailings-inspection-report-uk.html\">Tip and tailings inspection reports</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/mines-quarries-safety-audit-uk.html\">Mines and quarries safety audit</a></li></ul>",
